  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several zebrafish-based screening strategies by comparing untreated controls with drug-treated animals and linking each comparison to a typical experimental readout. Across the examples, the main idea is that treatment alters specific cells, tissues, signaling activity, or disease phenotypes, which can then be measured visually.\n\nIn embryos, one example shows loss of a particular cell population during development, detected by methods such as FISH or IHC. Another uses a p53-deficient background to create a sensitized screen, where drug treatment appears to trigger widespread cell death, with apoptosis assessed by TUNEL. A transgenic reporter line, labeled with dusp6:GFP, illustrates pathway monitoring: strong GFP signal in controls is reduced after treatment, indicating suppression of a signaling pathway such as MAPK.\n\nThe figure also includes tissue-restricted fluorescent reporters, such as lck:GFP in the thymus, where treatment causes disappearance or shrinkage of the labeled tissue. Finally, an adult zebrafish melanoma model with mitfa:GFP and BRAFV600E; p53 loss shows a large fluorescent tumor in controls and a much smaller one after treatment, representing a disease-phenotype readout. Overall, the image highlights how zebrafish screens can detect drug effects at multiple biological levels.",
